<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Trenton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Trenton was a short <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> battle fought on the morning of 26 December 1776, in Trenton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. After crossing the icy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> the previous night, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> led 2,400 men of the Continental Army against a garrison of about 1,400 Hessian auxiliaries commanded by Colonel Johann Rall. The Hessians, who had no long-distance outposts or patrols, were caught off guard; they lost 22 killed, including Rall, and 896 captured, while the Americans suffered two deaths on the march and five wounded. Coming after defeats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and a retreat through New Jersey, the victory raised the army's waning morale and inspired re-enlistments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-trenton.jpg" alt="Battle of Trenton" width="330" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by U.S. Government Printing Office; painting by Hugh Charles McBarron, Jr. (1902-1992) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Trenton_by_Charles_McBarron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In early December 1776 American morale was very low. The Americans had been ousted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> by the British and their Hessian auxiliaries and had retreated across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. Ninety percent of the soldiers who had served at Long Island were gone, and Washington wrote to his cousin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, &quot;I think the game is pretty near up.&quot; Some Hessian officers advised Colonel Johann Rall to fortify Trenton, and his engineers drew up plans for a redoubt, but he replied, &quot;Let them come... We will go at them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a>.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Washington planned attacks from three directions. John Cadwalader was to strike Bordentown, James Ewing was to seize the bridge over the Assunpink Creek, and a main force was to cross nine miles north of Trenton and attack in two columns under Nathanael Greene and John Sullivan. Sleet and snow delayed the crossing until 3:00 am, and Cadwalader and Ewing could not cross at all. Two men died on the march, and many soldiers wore rags on their feet. When Sullivan reported wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, Washington answered, &quot;Tell General Sullivan to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a>. I am resolved to take Trenton.&quot;</p><p>At about 8 am the Americans attacked a Hessian outpost. Artillery at the head of King and Queen streets broke the Hessian advances, and Sullivan's column blocked the crossing of the Assunpink Creek. Rall made a last attack on the American flank, but fire from three directions broke his regiments and he was mortally wounded. Most of the Hessians retreated into an orchard and surrendered; the Knyphausen regiment, cut off at the bridge, did so minutes later. The Americans took about 1,000 arms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and the garrison's provisions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Popular history often shows the Hessians drunk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> celebrations, but the historian Edward G. Lengel says there is no truth to the legend that they were helplessly drunk. Washington learned that Cadwalader and Ewing had not completed their crossing, so he lacked the force to attack Princeton and New Brunswick, and by noon his army was back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> with its prisoners. A week later came the Battle of the Assunpink Creek and the Battle of Princeton. The victory increased re-enlistments. Lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>, the future president, was badly wounded in the shoulder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Trenton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of Trenton</b> was fought on the morning of 26 December.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
